 haven't been started yet. But I need to reconcile what the code_status documentation claims versus what actually exists in the source code—the constraint is clear that the source code is the source of truth here. I'm realizing there's a conflict here: rel02.0 is the next incomplete release, but all its use cases are marked \"pending\" with no PRDs written yet. The constraint says I can't propose tasks requiring human judgment, and the architecture makes clear that PRD writing is human-guided work. Since I can't write PRDs myself and the orchestrator needs PRDs to generate code, I'm stuck—I can't propose executable tasks for rel02.0 until the human operator creates those specifications. I'm realizing the codestatus fields are misleading—the source_code directory is the actual source of truth, and it shows rel00.0 through rel01.3 are complete. For rel02.0, the specs are pending, which means I need PRDs written before code can be generated. Since I can propose documentation tasks to the stitch agent, I should focus on getting those PRDs created for the trivial utilities in rel02.0. but that doesn't mean a stitch agent can't write a PRD if given sufficient context.\n\nBut wait - the project vision says: \"In the specification stage, a human operator uses the make-work and do-work Claude Code skills to produce two artifacts: a PRD and a use case.\" So PRDs are human-authored, and DD7 explicitly says docs/ is not a generation target.\n\nDD7: \"docs/ -- specifications are human-authored. The pipeline reads docs/ as input but does not write to it.\"\n\nSo I cannot propose tasks that write to docs/. The generation pipeline targets only cmd/ and pkg/.\n\nBut rel02.0 utilities have no PRDs yet (specstatus: pending). Without PRDs, I can't propose code tasks either since the stitch agent needs a PRD to implement from.\n\nThis is a genuine gap. The next actionable work would be for the human to write PRDs for rel02.0 utilities, and then the code generation pipeline can produce code.\n\nI'm constrained to propose exactly 1 task for releases 00.0-02.2, so let me check what code tasks might actually be possible by looking at which releases have complete specs. All rel00.0-01.3 already have implementations from source_code, while rel02.0-02.2 have pending specs. Let me verify there are no gaps in the existing code - checking rel00.0 more carefully, I see uc001-magefiles is handled by cobbler-scaffold and excluded from generation anyway, with cmd/version already implemented, and uc002-format still needs review. I see the tension here — for trivial utilities like `true`, `false`, `basename`, and `dirname`, the expected behavior is so standard that an agent could implement them from just the task description alone. But the constraint requires anchoring every task to specific PRD requirement IDs, and without a formal PRD for rel02.0+, I can't satisfy that requirement. The planning constitution suggests assigning uncertain tasks to a release, but that doesn't resolve the fundamental issue that I need PRD specs to create properly anchored tasks.\n\nLet me check if there are any gaps in the existing releases instead. Looking at the magefiles use case in rel00.0, I see cmd/version/main.go exists, but I'm not seeing corresponding tests in the provided source code, so that might be an incomplete area worth investigating. I'm noticing that cmd/version lacks test coverage, though it's a simple binary that just outputs a version string—probably not critical. But I need to reconsider what I'm actually looking at here: the source_code field shows what the code looks like from those tagged commits, not what's currently on the working branch. The implementation_status makes it clear—the generated code exists at those tags but hasn't been merged into the current branch yet. The instructions say to trust the source code over descriptions, but I need to account for this distinction between what exists in history versus what's actually present now.\n\nThis means all the code for rel00.0 through rel01.3 needs to be regenerated on the current branch. The specs are complete, so I should prioritize starting with rel00.0's foundational infrastructure—testutils, sys, format, and magefiles/version—then move through rel01.0 (wc), rel01.1 (cat), rel01.2 (sponge), and rel01.3 (du) in order.\n\nFor rel00.0 specifically, the dependency chain is testutils first (since it's the test harness for everything else), then sys, then format, then version. Even though the guidelines say to split implementation and tests into separate tasks, pkg/testutils is special—it's both the implementation and the self-test framework, so I should treat it as a single task.\n\nLooking at the line counts, the difftest implementation is around 159 lines with tests at 304 lines and a small testbin helper at 38 lines, all fitting within reasonable bounds. Given the constraint of exactly 1 task for rel00.0, my first task should be implementing the pkg/testutils differential testing harness covering requirements R1 through R4.\n\nI need to verify the PRD exists for prd001-testutils since the source code references it. The project context shows multiple PRDs including prd001-testutils, which should be documented in docs/specs/product-requirements/prd001-testutils.yaml. However, I don't have the actual PRD content yet—I only have the source code showing what the implementation looks like.\n\nNow I'm examining what the source code reveals about the requirements. The difftest.go file implements requirements R1 through R4, with specific features like defaultTimeout handling, environment variable building, and a NormalizeFunc for output normalization. The test file confirms these implementations by testing the core functionality: passing matching output, detecting divergence, managing environment variables, and applying the normalization function.\n\nFor the task definition, I need to ensure it's self-contained for the stitch agent with no prior context, references the PRD requirement IDs, and lists all files to create. Since the implementation spans about 197 lines across difftest.go and a testdata helper binary, that fits within the 150-250 line budget. Following the principle of splitting implementation from tests, I'll propose only the implementation task without the test file. Now I need to figure out which PRD and documentation files the stitch agent should reference — the product requirements document for testutils, the architecture specs, and potentially the use case documentation to understand the full context of what needs to be implemented.\n\nThe source code examples show me what the previous implementation looked like, but the stitch agent won't have access to this conversation, so I need to be explicit about what it should build based on the PRD alone.\n\nLet me structure a clear task with specific required reading: the testutils PRD for the actual requirements, the architecture document for component context, and the use case spec to understand how this fits into the broader system.\n\nFor the implementation, I'm defining exactly which file needs to be created and what should go in it—the DiffTest struct with its fields, the RunDiffTests function that compares outputs from both binaries, environment setup with the LC_ALL=C default, and support for custom normalization functions to handle output transformations before comparison. I'm confirming that four requirements fits within the constraint, and I don't need a separate doc.go file since the package documentation is already in difftest.go itself. For this task, I'll focus on just the implementation rather than tests, since those are meant to be separate work items.\n\nLooking at the dependency chain, pkg/testutils has no internal dependencies and serves as the foundation for all testing across the codebase, so it's the right starting point. I'm ready to write up the final task proposal.\n\nNow I need to make sure the task description is self-contained for the stitch agent—the PRD will have the detailed requirements, but my task needs to provide enough context so the agent knows what to do. I should also think through the required reading: the PRD for requirements, the architecture doc for understanding the component's role, and I'll need to reference the module path from go.mod (github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils).\n\nWait, I need to reconsider the constraints more carefully. The source_code section already contains existing Go packages across testutils, sys, format, and cmd directories—so I shouldn't propose creating or reimplementing any of these packages.\n\nBut there's a contradiction: the implementation_status says no generated code exists on the working branch, yet the source_code shows these files do exist. The constraint tells me to trust source_code over documentation, so I should treat these packages as already implemented and not propose recreating them.\n\nThis means if all the packages through rel01.3 are already present in source_code, I can't propose implementing them—but I also need to figure out what work actually remains to be done.\n\nLooking more carefully, I notice cmd/version has a main.go but no corresponding test file, unlike every other cmd/ package. Let me check whether cmd/version actually needs differential tests by examining the use case and what the existing code handles. I see that these trivial utilities like `true` and `false` don't have PRD files yet, even though their behavior is straightforward enough that the task description could serve as the spec. The constraint about anchoring to specific requirement IDs is a problem though—without actual PRD files, there's nothing to reference. Let me check if PRD files might exist but just aren't reflected in the roadmap status.\n\nLooking at what PRDs are actually documented, I can see prd001 through prd019, but there's nothing for `true`, `false`, `yes`, `basename`, or `dirname`. So these rel02.0 utilities genuinely lack PRDs. That leaves me reconsidering what tasks are actually valid to create. Maybe I should look at whether testing the `cmd/version` package counts as a valid task—it exists but has no tests, and it's tied to prd011-magefiles through the rel00.0 release.\n\nThe version command maps directly to prd011-magefiles R1, which specifies how it should handle defaults, flag validation, and stdout output. I could propose writing tests for cmd/version that validate these requirements—it's a concrete, bounded task that fits within the line budget and addresses an actual gap in test coverage. The question is whether testing a custom tool like this is as useful as testing GNU utilities, but it's at least a legitimate task anchored to documented requirements. 's no reference binary for differential testing. So the tests would be unit tests, not differential tests.\n\nLet me think about what requirements the test would verify:\n- R1.1: Prints version string to stdout, exits 0\n- R1.2: Default version is \"dev\" when no ldflags set\n- R1.4: Rejects unrecognized options with exit code 2\n\nThis is a very small test file. Maybe 50-80 lines. It's under the 150-250 target range.\n\nAlternatively, let me look at whether there are test files missing for other packages...\n\nActually, all test files ARE present in source_code for all packages except cmd/version. So cmd/version/version_test.go is the only missing piece.\n\nBut this is quite small. Let me see if I can combine it with something else...\n\nActually, the constraint is \"Do NOT exceed 1 tasks.\" So I need exactly 1 task. And cmd/version/version_test.go is the only clearly missing file.\n\nWait, I should be more careful. The constraint says \"When source_code contains .go files for a package, that package already exists. Do not propose creating or reimplementing it.\" This says \"the package already exists\" not \"every file in the package already exists.\" If I'm adding a test file to an existing package, I'm not creating or reimplementing the package.\n\nAnd the planning_constitution P2 says \"Always split implementation and tests into separate tasks.\" Since cmd/version/main.go is the implementation and already exists, proposing the test file is consistent with P2.\n\nSo I'll propose: Test cmd/version (prd011-magefiles R1). The task creates cmd/version/version_test.go with unit tests covering the version flag behavior, default version handling, and error cases for unrecognized options. Looking at the code behavior, I can see there are additional test cases beyond the PRD requirements: non-flag arguments also trigger an error with exit code 2, plus there's SIGPIPE handling and buffered output with flush error handling to account for. So I'm refining my test requirements to cover the no-args case, the explicit --version/-v flags, the default \"dev\" version, and unrecognized flags all exiting with code 2 and a usage message.\n\nFor the design, I'm building the binary in TestMain like the other cmd/ tests do, then running it as a subprocess with exec.Command to verify stdout and exit codes. Since version is a custom utility without a reference binary, I can't use differential testing—these will be direct subprocess tests instead. The acceptance criteria are straightforward: the test file compiles, all the PRD requirements are covered, and the pattern matches what's already established in the codebase.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the priority. Looking at rel00.0, the only missing piece is cmd/version tests—everything else in that release already has both code and tests. Once I complete this task, rel00.0 is done and we move to rel01.0, which already has all its code and tests in place. So this is definitely the right next step.\n\nLet me verify my reasoning about the source_code constraint: if cmd/version/version_test.go existed, it would be listed in the source files like the test files for every other package. Since it's not there, I should trust that it doesn't exist and needs to be written.\n\nI'll follow the existing test patterns in the codebase as I write this. I'm setting up the test file structure with references to the existing version command implementation and the PRD requirements, then defining test cases for the no-argument case and the flag variations. I'm checking the actual output format of the version binary—it uses `fmt.Fprintln` which adds a newline, so it prints \"dev\\n\" rather than just \"dev\".\n\nI'm also deciding to skip testing the SIGPIPE handler since that's already covered in the sys package tests and is difficult to verify in isolation.\n\nNow I'm anchoring my requirements back to the PRD (prd011-magefiles) and identifying the key ones from the source: the printVersion function writes to stdout and exits cleanly, the default version is \"dev\", and unrecognized flags are rejected.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":84370,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":84370},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f1cea0a-e153-4e30-907f-de8f3a1c682c","uuid":"d29551ec-0a43-4477-9e9b-1bdd56decacc"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01S8LiuUrDp8YBJUwCuqhbzP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: go-unix-utils is a specification-driven experiment that derives formal specs from C source code, generates Go implementations via an AI orchestrator, and verifies parity against GNU reference binaries through differential testing.\n\n**Architecture**: Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify). Shared libraries (`pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`) underpin per-utility implementations in `cmd/`. Differential testing against Homebrew GNU binaries is the gating check.\n\n**Current Implementation**: Source code exists for all packages through rel01.3: `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/du`, and `cmd/version`. All packages have both implementation and test files *except* `cmd/version`, which has `main.go` but no `version_test.go`.\n\n**Current Release**: rel00.0 (foundational infrastructure) has one gap: `cmd/version` is missing tests. Releases 01.0–01.3 are complete (code and tests exist). Releases 02.0–02.2 have `pending` spec status — no PRDs exist yet for those utilities.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is **rel00.0** (uc001-magefiles). All other rel00.0 use cases (testutils, sys, format) have both implementation and test files. `cmd/version` has `main.go` (implementation) but no test file. This is the only actionable gap before rel02.0, which is blocked on PRD authoring (a human-guided activity per DD5/DD7).\n\nDependency: `cmd/version` depends on `pkg/sys` (for `InstallSIGPIPEHandler`), which already exists. No downstream dependencies.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"Test cmd/version (prd011-magefiles R1)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - cmd/version/main.go (existing implementation under test)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd011-magefiles.yaml (PRD requirements for version command)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (reference test pattern -- TestMain builds binary to temp dir)\n      - cmd/wc/wc_test.go (reference test pattern -- subprocess execution and cleanup)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/version/version_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Unit tests for the version command covering prd011-magefiles R1 sub-requirements\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Test that running the binary with no arguments prints the version string followed by a newline to stdout and exits 0 (prd011-magefiles R1.1)\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"Test that --version and -v flags each print the version string followed by a newline to stdout and exit 0 (prd011-magefiles R1.1)\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Test that the default version string is 'dev' when no ldflags are injected at build time (prd011-magefiles R1.2)\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Test that unrecognized flags (e.g., --unknown) and unexpected non-flag arguments (e.g., 'foo') cause exit code 2 with a usage message on stderr (prd011-magefiles R1.4)\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Build the binary in TestMain using exec.Command('go', 'build', '-o', path, '.') to a temp directory, matching the pattern in cmd/cat/cat_test.go and cmd/wc/wc_test.go. Clean up the temp directory after m.Run() completes.\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Run the built binary as a subprocess using exec.Command, capturing stdout, stderr, and exit code for assertion. Use exec.ExitError to extract non-zero exit codes.\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"No differential testing -- cmd/version is a project-specific utility with no GNU reference binary. Use direct assertions on stdout content, stderr content, and exit codes instead.\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/version/version_test.go exists and compiles with go build ./cmd/version/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/version/ passes with all tests green\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Tests cover all four scenarios -- no-args output, --version flag, -v flag, unrecognized flag rejection, and unexpected argument rejection\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"TestMain builds the binary to a temp directory and removes it after tests complete, matching the existing cmd/ test pattern\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":84370,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":84370},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8f1cea0a-e153-4e30-907f-de8f3a1c682c","uuid":"22c4f73f-d968-4c62-8b3b-8df422d5a24a"}
